{"qid":"Q235","names":["Principality of Monaco","Kingdom of Monaco"],"title":"Principality of Monaco","endonym":{"native":"Monaco","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"The Grimaldi family first took Monaco by stratagem in 1297 and intermittently held it thereafter, but the principality's continuous modern sovereignty is conventionally dated from 1612, when Honoré II assumed the title of Prince; 1642 marks his formal reception at the French court as a duc et pair étranger and the shift from Spanish to French protection. Monaco was annexed by Revolutionary France in 1793 and restored to the Grimaldis in 1814; the Congress of Vienna then placed it under Sardinian protection until France recognized full sovereignty in 1861, in exchange for which Charles III ceded the communes of Menton and Roquebrune. The abolition of income tax in 1869 and the expansion of the casino attracted wealthy European residents and transformed Monaco into a financial enclave; Rainier III (r. 1949–2005) and his successor Albert II continued this model, and the principality gained full UN membership in 1993.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Monaco's tiny enclave territory is precisely documented; its boundaries were fixed by the 1861 Franco-Monégasque Treaty and subsequent agreements.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Monaco","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Monaco"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Monaco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monaco"},{"label":"Britannica: Grimaldi family","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Grimaldi-family"}],"britannica":"place/Monaco","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Monaco","rulers":[{"qid":"Q382433